 * Writing internal logic before outlining all empty method signatures required by the problem statement.
 * Declaring collections without initializing them (= new ArrayList<>()), guaranteeing a NullPointerException at runtime.
 * Creating new entities but failing to save them to a central registry or data list before the method terminates.
 * Hardcoding entity IDs instead of implementing a static int auto-incrementing counter for unique generation.
 * Omitting relational fields (e.g., a ticket without an agentId), breaking the ability to trace ownership backward.
 * Restricting core operational methods with private access modifiers, preventing the client or main method from executing them.
 * Executing state changes on an object without verifying if the caller actually owns or has permission to modify that specific object.
 * Applying camelCase to class names (ticket, agent) instead of enterprise-standard PascalCase (Ticket, Agent).
